      "id": 1,
      "text": "The Cabin John Bridge Hotel, erected in 1870 beside the Union Arch Bridge, became a lavish hotspot for Washington’s elite.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabin_John%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Completed in 1864, the Union Arch Bridge was the world’s longest single‑span masonry arch at the time and remains the longest in the United States.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabin_John%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Gibson Grove earned its name from Sarah and Robert Gibson, freed African Americans who settled in lower Montgomery County after the Civil War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nps.gov/articles/gibson-grove-cabin-john-maryland.htm",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Cabin John Community Plan, launched in the 1960s, has been key to preserving the town’s distinctive way of life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
